---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="L. reuteri Yoghurt vs Probiotic Capsules & Kefir | Yoghurt of Youth"
  description="How live L. reuteri yoghurt compares to probiotic capsules and traditional ferments like kefir: named research-studied strains, delivered fresh and alive rather than dried or undefined."
>
  <section
    id="compare"
    class="relative scroll-mt-32 md:scroll-mt-24 bg-white text-slate-800 py-12"
  >
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-8">
      <h1 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900">
        L. reuteri Yoghurt vs Capsules and Traditional Ferments
      </h1>

      <p class="mt-4 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
        If you're trying to support your gut, you have broadly two options on the
        shelf. You can buy a probiotic capsule, which usually names its strains but
        arrives dried and dormant. Or you can buy a traditional ferment like kefir,
        which arrives genuinely alive but rarely tells you what's in it. Each solves
        half the problem. This page explains why we think you shouldn't have to
        choose, and what we built instead.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-slate-900">
        What a capsule gets right, and what it doesn't
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
        Probiotic capsules generally do the specific part well. They name their
        strains, they state a count, and many of those strains have real published
        research behind them. That's genuinely valuable, and we're not going to
        pretend otherwise.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
        The difficulty is the format. Probiotics are living organisms, and turning
        them into a shelf-stable powder means freeze-drying them, then storing them
        for months before anyone takes them. Freeze-drying is a genuinely harsh
        process: ice crystals and dehydration damage the cell membrane, and proteins
        and enzymes can be denatured along the way. Research on <em>L. reuteri</em>
        specifically has shown freeze-drying disrupting membrane structure and
        reducing enzyme activity. Cells that survive it are often what
        microbiologists call sublethally injured, meaning they are alive but have
        repairs to carry out before they function normally again. On rehydration
        they spend an extended lag phase doing exactly that, which delays the point
        at which they resume producing their metabolites.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
        A freshly fermented yoghurt has none of that to undo. The organisms have
        never been dried, never been stored for months, and are metabolically active
        when you drink them.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
        There is a second difference that follows from this, and we think it is the
        more important one. <strong>A ferment contains what the bacteria made.</strong>
        Over eight hours of fermentation, our <em>L. reuteri</em> doesn't just
        multiply, it works: producing lactic acid, exopolysaccharides, antimicrobial
        compounds, and breaking milk proteins into smaller bioactive peptides. These
        substances are an active area of research in their own right. A capsule
        delivers the organisms and nothing else. A ferment delivers the organisms
        <em>and</em> the environment they created, because that environment is the
        food itself.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
        The fermented milk also does something a capsule has to engineer around. Its
        protein and fat physically buffer the bacteria on the way through the
        stomach, which is one of the recognised reasons fermented dairy works well as
        a delivery vehicle. Capsules address the same problem with enteric coatings;
        a yoghurt comes with the solution built in.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
        There is a practical difference in scale, too. A capsule holds well under a
        gram of powder; a bottle of our yoghurt is 250ml of live ferment. That's why
        fresh ferments generally carry live culture counts well above what a capsule
        can physically hold.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
        And when the count is printed on a label, it isn't always what arrives. A
        2025 study from Newcastle University tested twelve commercial probiotics sold
        in the UK against their own labels, and found the products for human use
        varied in both species composition and viable count. Most fell within the
        researchers' accepted range, but one contained no detectable viable bacteria
        at all
        (<a href="https://doi.org/10.3390/microorganisms13081933" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="underline hover:text-amber-500 transition">Taha et&nbsp;al., 2025</a>).
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
        None of this is an argument that capsules don't work. Many have solid trial
        evidence behind them, and for convenience or travel they make sense. It is an
        argument that drying and shelf-storing a living thing introduces problems
        that fresh delivery simply doesn't have, and discards something a ferment
        gives you for free.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-slate-900">
        What a traditional ferment gets right, and what it doesn't
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
        Fermented foods have earned their reputation. Kefir, sauerkraut, kimchi,
        kombucha and traditional yoghurts all carry living microbes, and a varied
        intake of them is associated in research with a more diverse gut
        microbiome. We're genuinely fans of fermented foods, and nothing here is an
        argument against eating them.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
        They solve the format problem: the organisms are alive when you eat them.
        What they don't solve is the specificity problem. Kefir is fermented by a
        community of many different bacteria and yeasts, and the exact make-up
        varies from batch to batch and producer to producer. That diversity is part
        of the appeal, but it means you rarely know precisely which strains you're
        getting, or in what quantity, or whether this week's is the same as last
        week's.
      </p>

      <h3 class="mt-6 text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900">But isn't diversity the goal?</h3>
      <p class="mt-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
        This is the obvious objection to a single-strain approach, and it deserves a
        proper answer. A great deal of research does associate a diverse gut
        microbiome with better outcomes. We agree with that, and we are not arguing
        against it.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
        The distinction is between the <em>destination</em> and the
        <em>vehicle</em>. Diversity is a property of your gut, not a property that
        the thing you eat must also have. A single well-chosen strain can support the
        conditions in which a diverse microbiome flourishes, which is precisely what
        much of the research on specific strains examines. An undefined mixed culture
        is not automatically better at that, and it comes with the disadvantage that
        nobody knows what is in it. Precision is not the opposite of diversity. It is
        a more deliberate route to it.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
        It is also worth saying that a rotation gives you both. Over three weeks you
        receive three different well-studied strains, each delivered properly, rather
        than an unknown mixture all at once.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-slate-900">
        The third option
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
        Set those two side by side and the gap is obvious. A capsule is specific but
        not fresh, and delivers the organism alone. A traditional ferment is fresh and
        carries everything the culture produced, but you don't know what's in it. We
        built Yoghurt of Youth to be all three: specific, fresh, and complete.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
        Every batch we make is fermented with a single, named
        <em>L. reuteri</em> strain, and we tell you which one:
        <strong>DSM 17648</strong>, studied in connection with
        <em>Helicobacter pylori</em>; <strong>DSM 17938</strong>, studied for
        antipathogenic activity; and <strong>ATCC PTA 6475</strong>, studied in
        connection with the gut–brain axis. We rotate through them on a three-week
        cycle. Rather than dilute a strain within a crowd of other species, we
        isolate it and build the whole ferment around it.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
        It goes out alive. We ferment the day before dispatch and send it chilled, so
        it reaches you fresh rather than dried and stored for months. And because it
        is a ferment rather than a powder, it arrives with everything that strain
        produced over eight hours of fermentation, in the medium it produced it in.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
        If you want the reasoning behind the strain choices in more depth, our
        <a href="/theory" class="underline hover:text-amber-500 transition">theory of gut dysbiosis</a>
        sets it out, and our
        <a href="/story" class="underline hover:text-amber-500 transition">view on where gut health is heading</a>
        explains why we think this approach is the future.
      </p>

